{"hash":"a27f70d76dc42cef","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":93.872,"said":"Om Namah Shivaya. Come, walk with me across the river. Stop for a moment in the middle of the bridge and look down. That is the Narmada, one of India’s holiest rivers, flowing deep beneath your feet. Ahead of you she parts to flow around the hill-island of Mandhata, about four kilometres long, whose outline, people say, resembles the sacred syllable Om. That is why the Lord who lives there is called Omkareshwar, Lord of Om. Now look back, to the south bank behind you. There stands another temple, Mamleshwar, whose older name is Amareshwar, and pilgrims hold that the journey needs darshan of both. Its stones remember a great deal. Nearly a thousand years ago, in 1055, a Paramara king gave a whole village to Brahmins at ‘the holy Amareshvara’, and a few years later hymns to Shiva were carved on its walls. Centuries afterwards, Ahilyabai Holkar, the great queen of Indore, worshipped there, and priests of the Holkar family have performed its daily puja ever since. But why should one Lord dwell on two banks of the same river? For that we need the story of a proud mountain. Walk on, onto the island.\n\nDid you know? For centuries the only way across was by boat. A footbridge was opened in 1979, and the Mamleshwar Setu, 235 metres long, followed in 2004.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1110},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1126,"end":1265}],"words":[[0.098,0.204,0,2],[0.314,0.312,3,8],[0.639,0.553,9,16],[2.127,0.457,18,22],[2.764,0.325,24,28],[3.101,0.204,29,33],[3.317,0.132,34,36],[3.462,0.469,37,43],[3.942,0.096,44,47],[4.051,0.469,48,53],[5.409,0.385,55,59],[5.805,0.156,60,63],[5.974,0.036,64,65],[6.022,0.457,66,72],[6.49,0.096,73,75],[6.599,0.084,76,79],[6.695,0.373,80,86],[7.079,0.12,87,89],[7.212,0.096,90,93],[7.32,0.325,94,100],[7.656,0.192,101,104],[7.861,0.18,105,109],[8.053,0.421,110,114],[9.387,0.276,116,120],[9.675,0.108,121,123],[9.796,0.096,124,127],[9.9